<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Königsberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kaliningrad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Königsberg was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pregolya">Pregel</a> River near the Baltic coast, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in 1255 and named in honour of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>. It was the seat of the grand masters from 1457, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, coronation city of the Prussian kings and home of the university where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> taught. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945, it was renamed Kaliningrad and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konigsberg.jpg" alt="Königsberg" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nigsberg_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
